## 161

a. Factor x^2-4y^2=(x-2y)(x+2y). For x=2y, the second equation is
5y^2=20, giving (4,2),(-4,-2). For x=-2y it is y^2=20, giving
(-4sqrt(5),2sqrt(5)),(4sqrt(5),-2sqrt(5)).
b. The branches are x=3y/2 and x=-3y/2. Their second equations are
-y^2/4=-1 and 11y^2/4=-1 respectively. Only the first is real:
(3,2),(-3,-2).
c. Let p=xy. p^2+p-72=(p-8)(p+9)=0. With x+y=6, p=8 yields
(2,4),(4,2); p=-9 yields (3+3sqrt(2),3-3sqrt(2)) and its reversal.
d. Let s=x+y, p=xy. s^2-2s-15=0 gives s=5 or -3, and p=11-s.
For s=5,p=6 the coordinates are 2,3 in both orders. For s=-3,p=14,
the root discriminant 9-56=-47 excludes real solutions.

## 162

a. For original numerator m and denominator n, n is not 0,-3,-6.
(m+2)/(n+3)=m/n gives 2n=3m, so m/n=2/3.
(m+1)/(n+6)=2/3-1/6=1/2 gives 2m+2=n+6.
Thus m=8,n=12. The value is 2/3 but the original representation is 8/12.
b. n is not 0,-3,-1. 2(m+1)=n+3 gives n=2m-1.
5m(m-1)=n(n+1)=2m(2m-1), so m(m-3)=0.
m=0,n=-1 is inadmissible since n+1=0. The valid representation is 3/5.

## 163

a. Positive sides a,b obey a^2+b^2=289,ab=120. Then (a+b)^2=529,
a+b=23; t^2-23t+120=(t-8)(t-15). Sides 8 and 15 dm.
b. Legs obey a^2+b^2=676,ab=240 since area=ab/2. Their sum is
sqrt(676+480)=34, legs 10 and 24 cm, perimeter 34+26=60 cm.

## 164

a. Original legs a,b: a^2+b^2=169. New hypotenuse is 17, so
169+6(a+b)+18=289, a+b=17, ab=60, legs 5,12 m.
Area increase [(a+3)(b+3)-ab]/2=[3(17)+9]/2=30 m^2.
b. Smaller legs a,b: a^2+b^2=25. For larger legs a+17,b+17,
25+34(a+b)+578=841; a+b=7,ab=12, legs 3,4 dm.
Larger legs 20,21 dm. Area increase [17(7)+289]/2=204 dm^2.

## 165

a. Ordered solo times T1,T2>0 satisfy 1/T1+1/T2=1/40 and
T1/3+2T2/3=78. T1=234-2T2; T2^2-137T2+4680=0.
Roots T2=65,72 yield (T1,T2)=(104,65),(90,72), both positive
and satisfying both equations. No speed ordering is stated; both cases count.
b. 1/T1+1/T2=1/20 and T2/3=T1/2-3. Thus T2=3T1/2-9,
3T1^2-118T1+360=0, T1=36 or 10/3. The second gives T2=-4,
so only (36,45) hours is physically possible.

## 166

a. First speed u, second v, second pre-meeting time t; first time t+1.
First post-meeting duration 5/2 h, second 12/5 h (earlier arrival by 1/10 h).
Before-meeting distances: vt=(5/2)u, u(t+1)=(5/2)u+2=(12/5)v.
Hence t=3/2+2/u, v=(25u+20)/24 and 9u^2-32u-16=0.
u=4 or -4/9; negative excluded. v=5 km/h, t=2 h and distance 22 km.
Meeting is 3 h after first departure; arrivals at 5.5 and 5.4 h.
b. Bus u, motorcycle v, motorcycle pre-meeting time t.
Bus post-meeting duration 3/2 h; motorcycle duration 1 h.
u(t+1/2)=v and vt=(3/2)u. For r=v/u, r=3/(2r)+1/2,
(2r-3)(r+1)=0. r=3/2, and 90=(3/2)u+v=3u.
Bus 30, motorcycle 45 km/h; t=1 h.

## 167

a. 56 min=14/15 h, so u+v=112/(14/15)=120.
Train from A arrives first: 112/v-112/u=1/4. Substitution v=120-u
gives u^2+776u-53760=0, u=64 or -840.
Positive solution u=64,v=56 km/h; total times 1.75 and 2 h.
b. Let simultaneous pre-meeting duration t. Post-meeting times
tauA=1/3,tauB=3/4 h give vt=u/3 and ut=3v/4.
t^2=(1/3)(3/4)=1/4, so t=1/2; u/v=3/2.
u+v=80/(1/2)=160: A-to-B 96, B-to-A 64 km/h.

## 168

a. Expansion coefficients: x^3:1+ab=-9; x^2:4a+b-3=0;
x:4-3b=c. b=3-4a; 4a^2-3a-10=(a-2)(4a+5)=0.
(a,b,c)=(2,-5,19),(-5/4,8,-20). Both quadratic factors remain quadratic.
b. Expansion gives n=c+6+3q, zero x coefficient -3c-2q=0,
and cq=-24. q=-3c/2; c^2=16.
(n,c,q)=(-8,4,-6),(20,-4,6).

## 169

a. Isosceles leg l and base b; 2l+b=140. The inscribed rhombus
shares the base/leg vertex. Parallel sides and similarity give s/l+s/b=1,
so lb=24(l+b). Substitution yields l^2-82l+1680=0,
l=40 or 42, b=60 or 56 dm. Both have 2l>b>0 and s<l,b.
b. Rectangle height h and width w obey w=120(1-h/60)=120-2h.
h(120-2h)=1750 -> h^2-60h+875=0 -> h=25 or 35.
(w,h)=(70,25),(50,35) cm; perimeters 190 and 170 cm.
Both satisfy 0<h<60,w>0.

## 170

a. y >= x/3-4/3: included boundary line, region above.
b. y <= x-1/2: included boundary line, region below.
c. y >= |x| is intersection y>=x AND y>=-x, vertex (0,0).
d. y <= -|x| is intersection y<=x AND y<=-x, vertex (0,0).
e. y >= |x-2| is intersection y>=x-2 AND y>=2-x, vertex (2,0).
f. y <= |1-x|=max(1-x,x-1) is union y<=1-x OR y<=x-1,
vertex (1,0). All boundary rays are included; x can be any real number.

## 171

Slope OP=1/5, line 5y-x=0. All strictly upper points satisfy 5y-x>0;
all strictly lower points satisfy 5y-x<0 and are outside either interpretation.
The seed does not define whether "upper half-plane" contains its boundary.
Both {5y-x>0} and {5y-x>=0} are possible full sets.
DO NOT choose one. Retain an invariant question on line and strict interior.
Blocked source task: the unique full inequality and line-boundary inclusion.
Drawing production is also untested.

## 172

a. Upward parabola vertex (0,-4), y>=x^2-4, boundary included, above.
b. Downward parabola vertex (0,1), y<-x^2+1, boundary excluded, below.
c. y>=(x+2)^2, upward vertex (-2,0), boundary included, above.
All x are real.

## 173

a. y>=10/x: x!=0, same vertical direction on both branches.
b. xy>=10: x>0 -> y>=10/x; x<0 -> y<=10/x; x=0 impossible.
c. y<=4/x: x!=0, same vertical direction on both branches.
d. xy<=4: x>0 -> y<=4/x; x<0 -> y>=4/x; x=0 permits every y.
The hyperbola boundary is included wherever defined. Multiplication by a
negative x reverses inequality; division cannot decide the x=0 branch.

## 174

a. Entire affine line y=mx+c lies in y>3 only if m=0,c>3.
Nonzero slopes are unbounded below on all real x; y=4 is an example.
Vertical lines contain every real y and therefore also fail the whole-line condition.
b. Entire vertical quadratic graph y=ax^2+bx+c lies in y>5 iff a>0
and c-b^2/(4a)>5. Examples (x-1)^2+6 and (x+2)^2+6.
Equality of the vertex minimum to 5 is not sufficient; down-opening fails.
Writing any original example is a production task not demonstrated by selection.

## 175

Distance sqrt(x^2+y^2)<=7 iff x^2+y^2<=49. Closed disk centered at origin,
radius 7, including circumference and origin.

## 176

Sum of squares is nonnegative, and zero exactly at (0,0).
a: all R^2. b: R^2 without origin. c: singleton origin.

## 178

a: -3.7<x<3.7, arbitrary y: open vertical strip; neither vertical edge included.
b: y<=-5.5 OR y>=5.5, arbitrary x: two closed horizontal half-planes;
both boundary lines included. No finite restriction on the other coordinate.

## 179

a: closed diamond with vertices (+/-1,0),(0,+/-1), interior included.
b: outside/on diamond with vertices (+/-4,0),(0,+/-4); its interior excluded.
c: x<=0 allows every y; x>0 requires y>=x OR y<=-x. Boundary rays included.
d: y<=2|x| is union y<=2x OR y<=-2x; all y<=0 are included.
The rays have slopes +/-2; their shared vertex is (0,0), both included.

## 180

Diamond vertices (+/-2,0),(0,+/-2). Diagonals both length 4, perpendicular.
Area 4*4/2=8 square coordinate units, also four right triangles of area 2.

## 181

a. Intersection is open triangle, excluded edges and vertices:
(0,0),(2,4),(4,4). It exists only for 0<x<4 with x<y<min(2x,4).
b. Rewrite y<x+2,y>4-x,x<4. Open triangle with boundary vertices
(1,3),(4,0),(4,6); all excluded.
c. x>=0,7/2<=x+y<=6: unbounded closed half-band, all three boundaries included.
d. y>=1 and y+3<=x<=5-y imply y<=1; thus y=1,x=4: one point.
e. Closed rectangle [-2,2] x [-3,3], all four edges/vertices included.
f. [-4,4] x ((-infinity,-5] union [5,infinity)): two unbounded closed regions.

## 182

a. x=y=t>=0: northeast ray from origin, origin included.
b. x=-y, x<=0: (-t,t),t>=0: northwest ray, origin included.
c. x=y=t and 2t+2>=0: (t,t),t>=-1: northeast ray from (-1,-1),
starting point included. These are rays, not full lines or finite segments.

## 183

a. xy>=0: first/third closed quadrants and both full coordinate axes.
b. xy<=0: second/fourth closed quadrants and both full coordinate axes.
c. (x-2)(y-3)>=0: {x>=2,y>=3} union {x<=2,y<=3};
includes every point on x=2 and y=3.
d. (x+y)(x-y)=x^2-y^2<=0 iff |x|<=|y|; the two y-axis wedges,
including lines y=x and y=-x.
e. Domain x!=0,y!=0; product is (x+y)^2/(xy)>0 iff xy>0.
When xy>0 the numerator cannot vanish; opposite signs give nonpositive quotient.
Strict first/third quadrants, axes excluded.
f. Domain x!=0,y!=0; product is -(x-y)^2/(xy)>0 iff xy<0.
Opposite signs guarantee x!=y. Strict second/fourth quadrants, axes excluded.

## 184

3x+b<=y<=3x+1. b<1: nonzero-width closed parallel strip.
b=1: exactly line y=3x+1, not a nonzero-width strip.
b>1: empty. No arbitrary boundary convention is involved: both signs inclusive.

## 185

a. Closed annulus 1.3<=sqrt(x^2+y^2)<=3; both circles included.
b. Disk radius 6 intersect |x|>1. x in [-6,-1) union (1,6];
for each such x, -sqrt(36-x^2)<=y<=sqrt(36-x^2).
Lines x=+/-1 are excluded, including arc intersection points; allowed arcs included.
c. Disk radius 10 intersect -8<x<8; y between +/-sqrt(100-x^2).
All x=+/-8 points excluded, including (+/-8,+/-6); allowed arcs included.
d. Disk radius 1.2 in first/third closed quadrants; axes within disk included.
e. Annulus 8<=r<=10 in second/fourth closed quadrants; axes in annulus included.
f. x^2<=y<=9-x^2, -3/sqrt(2)<=x<=3/sqrt(2).
Meeting points (+/-3/sqrt(2),9/2), both curved boundaries included.
g. (x+2)^2<=y<=4, -4<=x<=0. Meeting points (-4,4),(0,4), boundaries included.
h. (x-1)^2<=y<=7/2, 1-sqrt(14)/2<=x<=1+sqrt(14)/2.
Meeting points (1+/-sqrt(14)/2,7/2); boundaries included.
i. (x-2)(x-3)<=y<=0, 2<=x<=3. Vertex (5/2,-1/4),
endpoints (2,0),(3,0) and all boundaries included.

## 187

a. Triangle (0,0),(-4,4),(4,4), base 8,height 4: area 16 square units.
b. On x<0 boundary y=-x intersects y=x/2+3/2 at (-1,1).
On x>=0 boundary y=x intersects at (3,3). Together with origin these
form a closed triangle. Determinant area |-1*3-1*3|/2=3 square units.
Both sloping branches matter; a horizontal-base formula using projection width
as the actual base would be invalid.

## Intended response design

Separate algebraic scenarios are independent, rather than linked numeric cells.
Sign/domain and geometry families can use complete-region comparison units.
Recognition cannot establish independent solving exposition, equation modeling,
graph drawing/shading, proof production or construction of original examples.
No source image is required by these seeds and no new diagram is essential to
the textual complete-region response units. Sources 177 and 186 are not selected.
171's convention-dependent full half-plane inequality stays expressly unfulfilled.
